#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059458 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059632 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8daba5bf-5162-5779-9512-f22d0c8c0320" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Piecewise Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "92d4072d-90d5-5b12-b043-9361efb446cd" :question/math-academy-id "e-1749" :question/problem "For the function $g(x)$ defined on the set of integers, find $g(-12)$ and $g(3),$ where $g(x)$ is the following piecewise function:\n![](images/43/43ec07dcf63fdaf3f85bd576c64da4f4afccee23143cfe51c337dd3e3e855cce.svg)" :question/worked-solution "- First, we compute $g(-12).$ Since $-12 < 1,$ we use the first branch:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & =2x \\\\ g(-12) & =2(-12) \\\\  & =-24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we compute $g(3).$ Since $3 \\geq 1,$ we use the second branch:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & =3x \\\\ g(3) & =3(3) \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059459 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059633 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a0df71e7-73ee-5b39-9c36-053215d90052" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Piecewise Step Function"}] :question/id #uuid "05e153cd-83bb-5d6a-b316-58835ee0fab2" :question/math-academy-id "e-1751" :question/problem "Given that\n![](images/cf/cfe7164dbaeba6215a7fcb84fd9bf192a19c6165562ef124344258df3fcafde6.svg)\nwhat is the graph of $y=f(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "We will graph each branch separately.\n\n- On the interval $(-\\infty, -3),$ we have $f(x) = 3,$ so the graph is a horizontal line that ends at $(-3,3),$ excluding this point.\n\n- On the interval $[-3, \\infty)$, we have $f(x) = 1,$ so the graph is a horizontal line that begins at $(-3,1),$ including this point.\n\nTherefore, the graph of the given function is as follows:\n\n![](images/f0/f0cdcbeb59ce6f011504ead0de090fa79c925fb933b1a99ce2f885e61eb33107.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059608 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059634 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bfe3639d-d134-5e2e-b405-a9285a33ec6c" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Piecewise Function"}] :question/id #uuid "c2b247d7-416c-5a41-99c2-b6afd3ca918a" :question/math-academy-id "e-6752" :question/problem "If $f$ is the piecewise function\n![](images/e6/e6a8c403594625a24b7f5af7eb5909d626e0ffbba5fc9a4947b7d856013c2bfe.svg)\nwhat is the graph of $y=f(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "We will graph each branch separately.\n\n- On the interval $(-\\infty, 0],$ we have $f(x) = -1$, so the graph is a horizontal line that ends at $(0,-1),$ including this point.\n\n- On the interval $(0, \\infty)$, we have $f(x) = - x+2$, so the graph is a straight line of slope $-1$ that starts from $(0,2),$ not including this point.\n\nTherefore, the graph of the given function is as follows:\n\n![](images/47/47a6d2d5b84424d0347efc7d9dc64ee90323855b233fde68827bff2a820121af.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059623 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059635 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec82342d-2986-5483-88f0-a0c85cac5110" :knowledge-point/title "The Domain of a Piecewise Function"}] :question/id #uuid "0ea16984-1ed3-51a5-b425-bfffea3bcd77" :question/math-academy-id "e-9628" :question/problem "What is the domain of the function shown below?\n\n![](images/2b/2b0235b4a984b1560312cef1c315ca8955bf062a90b75c3e03df8ee027e3cbd4.png)" :question/worked-solution "The function is defined for values between $-4$ and $1.$\n\n- Since there is an open circle at $x=-4,$ this point is not included in the domain.\n\n- Since there is a closed circle at $x=-1,$ this point is included in the domain.\n\n- Since there is an open circle at $x=1,$ this point is not included in the domain.\n\nSo, the function $f(x)$ above is defined for values between $x=-4$ and $x=1,$ not including $x=-4$ and $x=1$ but including $x=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\nx\\in (-4,1)\n$$\n\nNote that the function is defined at the point $x=-1,$  even though the function jumps at that point. The function value at $x=-1$ is $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506576 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059635 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec82342d-2986-5483-88f0-a0c85cac5110" :knowledge-point/title "The Domain of a Piecewise Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04cd771e-a59d-585b-9a33-55f846dde1fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505926} {:answer/id #uuid "149821a9-bec8-5cdb-92ab-f25f3f93a897" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (2,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505951} {:answer/id #uuid "503def74-391f-5205-b6c9-9b120f1146d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-2,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506047} {:answer/id #uuid "81c28162-d952-55d8-8172-a7bc88b018ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (2,3]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506132} {:answer/id #uuid "de1348e2-9cc2-5dc2-b2de-ddf758c41819" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,3]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506309}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "04cd771e-a59d-585b-9a33-55f846dde1fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505926} :answer-field/id #uuid "74dfdc8c-1de1-5138-8640-f866d354fd8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506433}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ad1cae5c-dea6-50a2-9c54-2a5d56edb7d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-129286" :question/problem "![](images/19/1969166670ad908b426c49b4f302a1b56b17693a09d97ddcd64c40ba5dc44987.png)\n\nWhat is the domain of the function $f(x),$ shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The function is defined for values less than $-2,$ and for values between $-2$ and $3.$\n\n- Since there is a closed circle at $x=-2,$ this point is included in the domain.\n\n- Since there is an open circle at $x=3,$ this point is excluded from the domain.\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\nx\\in (-\\infty ,3).\n$$\n\nNote that the function is defined at the point $x=-2,$  even though the function jumps at that point. The function value at $x=-2$ is $1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060191 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059634 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bfe3639d-d134-5e2e-b405-a9285a33ec6c" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Piecewise Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "abb454b4-6d74-5119-85f4-a178c7af259e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The graph is not a function" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057608} {:answer/id #uuid "0df10c74-c1ce-5a90-98c4-8fba09a65fd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+1,\\, & x\\le 1 \\\\ -2-2x,\\, & x>1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505938} {:answer/id #uuid "dfa718ca-5e40-5093-8ed6-1a6926b999dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-1,\\, & x\\le 1 \\\\ 1-2x,\\, & x>1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506312} {:answer/id #uuid "e56d03f3-6214-5a2d-b812-62eb1213aa8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+1,\\, & x\\le 1 \\\\ 2-2x,\\, & x>1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506329} {:answer/id #uuid "e6676b49-b44c-580f-b15e-a5e689da79a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-x,\\, & x\\le 1 \\\\ x-1,\\, & x>1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506331}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e56d03f3-6214-5a2d-b812-62eb1213aa8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+1,\\, & x\\le 1 \\\\ 2-2x,\\, & x>1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506329} :answer-field/id #uuid "070fe48e-347f-5977-a780-ed4db2b88659" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057606}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2551a862-e523-555f-9254-175cc890352b" :question/math-academy-id "q-19304" :question/problem "![](images/1a/1a7b132b3efcd909ec61421c956c6808cdbfd813123e2e9341fd0116d0d3deda.png)\n\nWhat is the definition of the function $y=f(x)$ shown above?" :question/worked-solution "We will graph each branch separately.\n\n- On the interval $(-\\infty ,1],$ we have a line with slope $2$ and $y$-intercept $1.$ So the graph is the line $y=2x+1$ that ends at $(1,3),$ including this point.\n\n- On the interval $(1,\\infty )$, we have a line with a slope of $-2$ that passes through the point $(1,0).$ We can find the equation of this line using point-slope form:\n\n$$\ny-0=-2(x-1)\\,⟹\\,y=2-2x\n$$\n\nSo the graph is the line $y=2-2x$ that starts at $(1,0),$ excluding this point.\n\nTherefore, the function is defined as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+1,\\, & x\\le 1 \\\\ 2-2x,\\, & x>1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506601 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059632 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8daba5bf-5162-5779-9512-f22d0c8c0320" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Piecewise Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "150af1db-ec30-5f2f-88b3-80195d4642a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505953} {:answer/id #uuid "3d32363b-51ca-58e6-95b2-caf5fe68f209" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506012} {:answer/id #uuid "3f1a4b4b-e61a-541c-a150-e2e138cfb3bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506015} {:answer/id #uuid "82a694fe-810e-5de5-8a45-37cf9b7d272d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506136} {:answer/id #uuid "99a844c9-c435-5475-9100-27bc9c6a8999" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506180}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f1a4b4b-e61a-541c-a150-e2e138cfb3bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506015} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb886fa5-107b-56e6-a6e2-1536f8ff860d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506480}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fea5a4c0-e40e-5131-a0df-cb2f1f77a5c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-19307" :question/problem "Given the following piecewise function $f(x),$ what is $f(1)?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+7,\\, & x\\le 1 \\\\ 2x-3,\\, & x>1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since $x=1$ is in the interval $x\\le 1,$ we use the left-hand branch, namely $f(x)=2x+7:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =2(1)+7 \\\\  & =2+7 \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060275 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059635 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec82342d-2986-5483-88f0-a0c85cac5110" :knowledge-point/title "The Domain of a Piecewise Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ffeea7e-6d1a-5f6e-8d9d-08672465c5cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (-1,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506016} {:answer/id #uuid "92c2451b-a10e-5360-af69-be02f0a0f8ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (-1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506159} {:answer/id #uuid "b1f13cb6-9a35-5892-8bcb-eb8cec537b8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,4]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506235} {:answer/id #uuid "e1f1cb7d-01ea-52fc-bab0-d5f108f577bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4,-1)\\cup (-1,4]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506323} {:answer/id #uuid "f0aa27c8-c3d8-525c-ad48-4859d71bce3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (-1,4]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506355}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0aa27c8-c3d8-525c-ad48-4859d71bce3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (-1,4]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506355} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b3336fc-3ab6-5f17-8fab-6bf06fa9d81c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058272}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5fce58b4-e9bb-50e8-aefb-7f6df598ae68" :question/math-academy-id "q-34612" :question/problem "![](images/48/4822b0bf0e9bf1d2ac7d6410f2f1768c35a5504188eb58581d6f723772e5bb0a.png)\n\nWhat is the domain of the function shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The function is defined  for values less than $-1,$ and between $-1$ and $4.$\n\n- Since there is a closed circle at $x=4,$ this point is included in the domain.\n\n- On the other hand, because there are two open circles for $x=-1,$ this point is not included in the domain.\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\nx\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (-1,4].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506574 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059633 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a0df71e7-73ee-5b39-9c36-053215d90052" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Piecewise Step Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b04d53c-71b3-5fa8-971b-fe5342b88f7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1\\,,\\, & x<0 \\\\ 0\\,,\\, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506069} {:answer/id #uuid "a9025efa-a52c-5478-94f4-dbd3e93c91d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0\\,,\\, & x\\le 1 \\\\ 1\\,,\\, & x>1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506220} {:answer/id #uuid "ae5e0509-dedd-5266-aadb-db54ab0a2371" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0\\,,\\, & x<0 \\\\ 1\\,,\\, & x>0\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506228} {:answer/id #uuid "e07ea9ec-c822-5f9d-a277-c86ec6c40658" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0\\,,\\, & x\\le 0 \\\\ -1\\,,\\, & x>0\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506316} {:answer/id #uuid "e471feac-5100-522d-8fca-7b25ced37a7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-1\\,,\\, & x<1 \\\\ 0\\,,\\, & x\\ge 1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506328}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9025efa-a52c-5478-94f4-dbd3e93c91d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0\\,,\\, & x\\le 1 \\\\ 1\\,,\\, & x>1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506220} :answer-field/id #uuid "656b0233-0768-5523-842d-621f0a5ed5aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506425}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a93d97c6-5ea5-55ad-8efc-6abdfa388b3a" :question/math-academy-id "q-46319" :question/problem "![](images/51/51558d572afe9611d7bbd49e63f71691ae4b8ed386cfa49c2d38817cd8bc7e30.png)\n\nWhat's the definition of the function $y=f(x)$ shown above, given that $f(x)$ is defined for $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )?$" :question/worked-solution "We will determine each branch separately.\n\n- On the interval $(-\\infty ,1],$ we have $f(x)=0.$ Notice that the point $x=1$ is included.\n\n- On the interval $(1,\\infty )$, we have $f(x)=1.$ Notice that the point $x=1$ is excluded.\n\nHence the definition of $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0\\,,\\, & x\\le 1 \\\\ 1\\,,\\, & x>1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506586 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059633 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a0df71e7-73ee-5b39-9c36-053215d90052" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Piecewise Step Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "403faf95-59a7-5c3a-bb47-2d08b5e3cc94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3\\,,\\, & x<-2 \\\\ -2\\,,\\, & -2\\le x<1 \\\\ 2\\,,\\, & x\\ge 1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506017} {:answer/id #uuid "5d58d27e-db4f-5504-993b-b1e1e0f7bd8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2,\\, & x<-2 \\\\ -1,\\, & -2<x<1 \\\\ 3,\\, & x>1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506072} {:answer/id #uuid "9e0ce3f2-a3f4-5c23-9872-19e3234a5167" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3\\,,\\, & x<-1 \\\\ -2\\,,\\, & -1<x<2 \\\\ 2\\,,\\, & x>2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506190} {:answer/id #uuid "fd1573cc-7433-5e78-8f6b-ed17c3a96319" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-3,\\, & x<-2 \\\\ 2,\\, & -2\\le x<1 \\\\ 1,\\, & x\\ge 1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506375} {:answer/id #uuid "ff2f9234-d84f-55a4-b4b7-ad2e213d7a1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3,\\, & x\\le -2 \\\\ 2,\\, & -2<x\\le 1 \\\\ -1,\\, & x>1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506377}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "403faf95-59a7-5c3a-bb47-2d08b5e3cc94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3\\,,\\, & x<-2 \\\\ -2\\,,\\, & -2\\le x<1 \\\\ 2\\,,\\, & x\\ge 1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506017} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddb40709-0719-5c23-9c70-549b0439b7d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506487}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c6e693a9-56de-5225-8098-edeb2da1b200" :question/math-academy-id "q-46373" :question/problem "![](images/aa/aa047bf7762739d439b076816b9f25dde4fdd632df08a2eb8e6424803f03a91c.png)\n\nWhat's the definition of the function $y=f(x)$ shown above, given that $f(x)$ is defined for $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )?$" :question/worked-solution "We will determine each branch separately.\n\n- On the interval $(-\\infty ,-2),$ we have $f(x)=3.$ Notice that the point $x=-2$ is excluded.\n\n- On the interval $[-2,1),$ we have $f(x)=-2.$ Notice that the point $x=-2$ is included and the point $x=1$ is excluded.\n\n- On the interval $(1,\\infty )$, we have $f(x)=2.$ Notice that the point $x=1$ is included.\n\nHence the definition of $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3\\,,\\, & x<-2 \\\\ -2\\,,\\, & -2\\le x<1 \\\\ 2\\,,\\, & x\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506531 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059634 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bfe3639d-d134-5e2e-b405-a9285a33ec6c" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Piecewise Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28b5c02c-67c8-5f06-b143-6742ea487b89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3x,\\, & x\\le 3 \\\\ 2,\\, & x>3\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505981} {:answer/id #uuid "2fdf7af2-1473-5b37-8372-f5c15d05327e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2,\\, & x\\le 3 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}x,\\, & x>3\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505996} {:answer/id #uuid "a3186160-228b-5740-8f76-391631f415ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{1}{3}x,\\, & x<1 \\\\ 2,\\, & x\\ge 1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506206} {:answer/id #uuid "ab05b8e7-842e-557a-a1a9-5751fcb11128" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}x,\\, & x\\le 3 \\\\ 2,\\, & x>3\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506223} {:answer/id #uuid "cfd3cd9f-4bfa-5684-a8e1-f95a5c6e4ecf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x,\\, & x\\le 1 \\\\ 2,\\, & x>1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506283}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab05b8e7-842e-557a-a1a9-5751fcb11128" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}x,\\, & x\\le 3 \\\\ 2,\\, & x>3\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506223} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfca57a4-a336-5739-bc9f-c538ba48cda4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506492}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2e9f0d78-2c67-580d-a52c-3d6cdded2112" :question/math-academy-id "q-7289" :question/problem "![](images/d1/d16451ba3da7b703a6b5a07e3ddcda7995c20dae4a4724382f1519017c719245.png)\n\nWhat is the definition of the function $y=f(x)$ shown above?" :question/worked-solution "We will graph each branch separately.\n\n- On the interval $(-\\infty ,3],$ we have a line with a slope of $\\frac{1}{3}$ and $y$-intercept $0.$ So the graph is the line $y=\\frac{1}{3}x$ that ends at $(3,1),$ including this point.\n\n- On the interval $(3,\\infty ),$ we have a horizontal line that passes through the point $(3,2).$ So the graph is the line $y=2$ that starts at $(3,2),$ excluding this point.\n\nTherefore, the function is defined as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}x,\\, & x\\le 3 \\\\ 2,\\, & x>3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506523 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059632 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8daba5bf-5162-5779-9512-f22d0c8c0320" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Piecewise Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4356f7e1-d020-5df5-9671-0b5ffb441e32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506025} {:answer/id #uuid "a8f15e85-55b4-56fb-9bd6-07b79dfdd15c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506219} {:answer/id #uuid "cc643682-a43f-5de3-b685-eae1e0e9fba4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506273} {:answer/id #uuid "e950dc80-9831-521e-93e7-d728826cb5cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506338} {:answer/id #uuid "ea83d4e6-58b8-5f78-b35d-5f15e5936d7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506339}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8f15e85-55b4-56fb-9bd6-07b79dfdd15c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506219} :answer-field/id #uuid "5283cc23-d439-52da-a0b1-22daa8ef3c68" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506412}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1fbabf96-94ef-591d-8e8e-acb10250f475" :question/math-academy-id "q-7302" :question/problem "Given the following piecewise function $f(x),$ what is $f(5)?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4,\\, & x\\le 5 \\\\ 5,\\, & x>5\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since $x=5$ is in the interval $x\\le 5,$ we use the left-hand branch, namely $f(x)=4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(5) & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
